    {
      "question": "e-21120",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "8b20780e39c32ab4e4bd392f27af13d2d99572614ea09c1d857ba659d3fffc70",
      "new_sha256": "4d7697cfd3dace8e2870aee2f78fbb07695a6330df3d97214bb716c87af9b348",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-21120",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/example-21120.json",
          "file_sha256": "4895b8af5fdad2276b6867d89255426f841cc845a14eee04881d6a1e5d72fc39"
        }
      ],
